  "problem": "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{81}{4})+2=(y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{81}{4} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+9y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{81}{4})+2 & = \\\\ {(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
